DO-WHILE LOOPS

do-while loops execute a line of code so long as a condition is met, this means that unless the condition itself has a way to be measured or counted, the loop will go on an indefinite amount of times.

Its syntax is the following:

  do
  {
  //code
  }
  while (condition);

EX:

bool isASuperCoolBoolean = True;

  do
  {
  Console.WriteLine("Cheese");
  }
  while(isASuperCoolBoolean == True);

  //This would print an infinite number of "Cheese" strings. World hunger be damned.

You should be wary not to utilize this loop if the action you want to repeat has to be done only a set number of times! These loops are helpful for shutting down sections of code to be activated when a
Boolean switch is flipped (for example) or to create a small vacuum for repeated variables. !: All variables defined inside of a loop are exclusively used in said loop, be wary of not using nested variables!
They wont be able to be found. Think of it like a sealed off chamber. Whatever happens in it, stays in. Also beware to not confuse these with while loops! Those are different.

Once I got to the fast-food exercise we did that used these a lot they clicked really quickly. They're specially helpful for the scope they provide in allowing repeated variables.

